1
00:00:00,046 --> 00:00:08,480
If someone was to make a fully functioning quantum computer tomorrow, then at least at the moment we wouldn't have all that much we could do with it.

2
00:00:08,686 --> 00:00:15,908
I left quantum computing in 2020 after finishing an applied maths PhD in that field from Cambridge University.

3
00:00:16,302 --> 00:00:26,404
And the thing is, I loved being a researcher, and I even got offered a few postdocs kind of informally when I was thinking about leaving, but I still decided to leave.

4
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:36,240
and the reason is because I became a little bit concerned that maybe quantum computers aren't going to be as useful in the real world as we might have hoped.

5
00:00:36,430 --> 00:00:39,780
Well, it's been five years and a lot has happened in quantum computing.

6
00:00:40,014 --> 00:00:46,640
So I want to go back and see if my assessment from back then is really still looking like it's going to be true.

7
00:00:46,958 --> 00:00:50,720
So yeah, on the hardware front, I think things are actually going really well.

8
00:00:50,958 --> 00:00:56,772
In 2020, when I left, most quantum computing companies only had like dozens of qubits.

9
00:00:57,038 --> 00:00:59,760
I think the state of the art might have been like around 50.

10
00:00:59,920 --> 00:01:02,560
But now there are companies that have like 10 times that.

11
00:01:02,894 --> 00:01:10,052
And what's even more impressive is that most companies seem to be on track with what they said they would be able to do in 2025.

12
00:01:10,240 --> 00:01:22,720
I mean, I'm not an expert in that area, but it seems like despite all the incredible challenges engineering-wise to make it happen, these companies are doing it, which is awesome.

13
00:01:23,022 --> 00:01:32,068
I have no idea when fully functioning quantum computers will be built, but some people say 10 years or 20 years, and I have no reason not to believe that.

14
00:01:32,270 --> 00:01:34,084
I think that we really are on track.

15
00:01:34,400 --> 00:01:38,880
But on the software front, I think things have been much more disappointing.

16
00:01:39,182 --> 00:01:43,460
To explain why, I need to explain one of the most misunderstood things about quantum computers.

17
00:01:43,822 --> 00:01:47,716
Quantum computers are not just general purpose supercomputers.

18
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:53,988
It's not like every single thing that you do on a regular computer can be done faster on a quantum computer.

19
00:01:54,286 --> 00:01:57,092
Like you can't play games faster on a quantum computer.

20
00:01:57,326 --> 00:02:02,116
In fact, quantum computers are very specialized at doing very specific tasks.

21
00:02:02,446 --> 00:02:06,916
And I want to explain a little bit about quantum computers so you can see why that is.

22
00:02:07,534 --> 00:02:23,780
I'm not going to explain what a qubit is or anything like that, because if you want to see a full explanation of like exactly how a quantum computer works, then I have a video that you can watch where I built a small, kind of useless quantum computer at home, and hopefully that explains it a little bit better.

23
00:02:24,206 --> 00:02:31,520
But right now, what I want to do is give you an overview of like how you should be thinking about what a quantum computer can do and what it can't do.

24
00:02:31,758 --> 00:02:35,236
Let's say that this is a normal computer that does this algorithm.

25
00:02:35,630 --> 00:02:45,220
Every time you put in a bit string, if I put that into my computer, I type it in, then it will output some other bit string.

26
00:02:45,454 --> 00:02:51,172
There's some sort of computation that this program is doing to the bit string to make it something else.

27
00:02:51,982 --> 00:02:58,000
Well, what happens when you replace this computation with the quantum version of this computation?

28
00:02:58,240 --> 00:03:02,660
So let's say this is my amazing quantum computer.

29
00:03:03,214 --> 00:03:08,320
It acts in a very similar way, where if you put in this bit string, you'll get out another bit string.

30
00:03:08,718 --> 00:03:15,908
But the thing that you can do with quantum computers that you can't with a classical computer is you can put in more than one input at a time.

31
00:03:16,110 --> 00:03:27,940
In fact, you can put in every possible bit string, and the output, instead of being a single number, will be a combination of all of the outputs for every single one of the inputs.

32
00:03:28,334 --> 00:03:29,840
This seems miraculous, right?

33
00:03:30,030 --> 00:03:39,520
Like you can do an exponential amount of computation with the same amount of resources that a regular computer would take to just do one computation.

34
00:03:39,760 --> 00:03:43,428
This seems like there needs to be some sort of catch, and there is.

35
00:03:43,790 --> 00:03:51,524
You see, it is true that a quantum computer truly does do this whole amount of computation all at the same time.

36
00:03:52,078 --> 00:03:57,120
But the problem is when you try and ask the quantum computer what the answer is.

37
00:03:57,614 --> 00:04:03,684
So, with a regular computer, you might expect it just outputs this whole thing in a way that's readable.

38
00:04:04,238 --> 00:04:08,708
But with a quantum computer, you have to then go and measure this output.

39
00:04:09,102 --> 00:04:15,012
And when you measure it, what happens is it collapses to just one of these things randomly.

40
00:04:15,342 --> 00:04:22,468
So all the rest is completely gone, and you just have one number that comes out and it's just a random computation.

41
00:04:22,894 --> 00:04:24,304
And that is not useful.

42
00:04:24,494 --> 00:04:41,984
You see, even though technically a quantum computer really does do a huge amount of parallel computation at once with the same cost that a regular computer would take to just do one computation, it's not possible to easily extract all that information.

43
00:04:42,286 --> 00:04:45,744
In fact, you can extract very little from the output.

44
00:04:45,934 --> 00:04:50,980
And if you do it in the naive way of just trying to look at the answer, you just get a random answer.

45
00:04:51,344 --> 00:04:54,340
So, how are quantum computers ever going to be useful then?

46
00:04:54,624 --> 00:05:07,744
Well, it turns out for very particular types of computations that it is possible to extract just slightly more information from this whole huge list that lets you actually get out something very useful.

47
00:05:07,918 --> 00:05:15,104
This is my second sponsorship for the charity FarmKind, and they told me that last time loads of you donated, which is so awesome.

48
00:05:15,264 --> 00:05:16,132
Thank you so much.

49
00:05:16,718 --> 00:05:26,544
So almost no one thinks it's okay for chickens to live in horribly cramped cages like this for their entire lives where they can barely even move, but it's still really common.

50
00:05:26,784 --> 00:05:36,784
This report from June of 2025 by the USDA says that about 55% of all egg-laying hens are in cages like these.

51
00:05:37,134 --> 00:05:42,064
The mission of Farmkind is to prevent these kind of cruelties in the most effective way possible.

52
00:05:42,224 --> 00:05:50,404
They research which charities have actually made the biggest difference for animals, and then when you donate to Farmkind, they split that money amongst these charities.

53
00:05:50,798 --> 00:05:52,836
So, for example, the Humane League.

54
00:05:53,024 --> 00:05:59,744
They run campaigns that have pressured the likes of McDonald's, Walmart, and Nestle to treating their hens better.

55
00:06:00,046 --> 00:06:08,388
Their track record is so good that they can prevent a chicken from living her entire life in one of these terrible cages for just 85 cents.

56
00:06:09,198 --> 00:06:16,580
It often feels like we can't change the world, but for the individual animals that your donations will help, it makes all of the difference.

57
00:06:17,134 --> 00:06:21,380
So if you want to donate, the link is in the description and I would really appreciate it.

58
00:06:21,870 --> 00:06:23,108
Now back to the video.

59
00:06:23,630 --> 00:06:27,344
So let me give you the classic example, Shaw's factoring algorithm.

60
00:06:27,630 --> 00:06:31,044
So the problem that Shaw's algorithm is trying to solve is called factoring.

61
00:06:31,438 --> 00:06:40,452
So say I give you this massive number, then the factoring problem asks, what are the two numbers that you have to multiply to get that massive number?

62
00:06:40,686 --> 00:06:43,652
In other words, can you factorize this number?

63
00:06:44,078 --> 00:06:47,396
And it turns out this is really hard to do on a classical computer.

64
00:06:47,566 --> 00:06:54,244
Like, yes, you can do it by sort of just checking every single factor, but that will take you ages and ages.

65
00:06:54,478 --> 00:07:02,372
But in 1999, Peter Shaw showed that actually you can solve this problem quite quickly on a quantum computer.

66
00:07:02,638 --> 00:07:11,780
The first step of that algorithm does look like putting in a huge superposition of all the inputs into a quantum computer and calculating the output.

67
00:07:12,144 --> 00:07:20,132
But the next step is not to just go and measure this, because as we already saw, doing that just collapses the state and is totally useless.

68
00:07:20,622 --> 00:07:27,812
So instead, Shaw's algorithm uses a very clever way of manipulating this state further.

69
00:07:28,270 --> 00:07:48,720
So you put this back into Another quantum computer doing a whole lot more steps, and you end up with some sort of quantum state here where now it is possible to measure this state and actually get out a little bit of useful information.

70
00:07:48,974 --> 00:07:53,520
and that is just enough to find out how to factorize that huge number.

71
00:07:53,838 --> 00:08:07,556
But the point is you have to come up with some very clever manipulation at this part of the quantum computer, a particular algorithm that is going to take out just the useful bit of information so that at the end you can read out what that information is.

72
00:08:08,110 --> 00:08:14,084
But designing this algorithm is hard and it depends exactly what the problem you want to solve is.

73
00:08:14,382 --> 00:08:18,560
And some problems, like factoring, have turned out to be amenable.

74
00:08:19,054 --> 00:08:30,244
So it turns out there are a bunch of problems like that where we have been able to come up with some very clever quantum algorithm that does let us extract the information we want and solve the problem.

75
00:08:30,766 --> 00:08:37,200
But for most hard problems that you might want to solve with a quantum computer, we haven't yet found quantum algorithms.

76
00:08:37,520 --> 00:08:47,844
That might be because no one has yet found it, or it could be that there is no algorithm to find that quantum computers just can't solve that class of problem.

77
00:08:48,366 --> 00:08:54,116
And we never know which it is until someone eventually finds a quantum algorithm for that problem.

78
00:08:54,638 --> 00:08:57,604
So you can see why quantum algorithm design is quite tricky.

79
00:08:57,840 --> 00:09:02,948
You really never know which problems you're going to be able to solve with a quantum computer or not.

80
00:09:03,438 --> 00:09:14,820
When Shaw's algorithm came out in 1994, there was a lot of enthusiasm that we would be able to find many more problems like factoring that quantum computers would be able to do so much better than classical computers.

81
00:09:15,120 --> 00:09:22,116
And yet, by the time I was leaving my PhD in 2020, I was pretty disappointed by the lack of progress.

82
00:09:22,414 --> 00:09:37,508
There were, of course, other quantum algorithms, like for example phase estimation, which is actually very related to Shaw's factoring algorithm, and also another one called Hamiltonian simulation, which is quite different and allows you to simulate quantum objects.

83
00:09:37,774 --> 00:09:48,772
But now, five years later, I want to go back and see if, well, there's any new algorithms, but also whether there have been new applications found for the existing algorithms.

84
00:09:49,200 --> 00:09:55,556
And the spoiler is, I think it's been a little bit disappointing, but there have been some hopeful signs.

85
00:09:56,014 --> 00:09:58,116
Let's start with quantum machine learning.

86
00:09:58,414 --> 00:10:03,120
So we've all seen how incredibly good AI systems are.

87
00:10:03,310 --> 00:10:12,356
And I think there's this worry that sometimes gets brought up in the media that when we have full quantum computers, we're going to have these supercharged AIs.

88
00:10:12,686 --> 00:10:20,996
In fact, when I was in the field, quantum machine learning was super popular, and there seemed to be papers being churned out about it all the time.

89
00:10:21,294 --> 00:10:31,460
But I was very, very skeptical that any of those would amount to much because I just don't think that quantum computers are very well suited to machine learning problems.

90
00:10:31,854 --> 00:10:46,400
Quantum computers often exploit structure, and you know, machine learning algorithms are all about putting in tons of data, and the data isn't particularly structured, it's like whatever examples, for example, from the internet.

91
00:10:46,880 --> 00:10:50,148
So I just don't think this is playing to quantum computers' strength.

92
00:10:50,640 --> 00:10:56,644
And it seems like more and more people have gone off this quantum machine learning idea.

93
00:10:57,198 --> 00:10:59,204
So I don't think that's going to be a big deal.

94
00:10:59,760 --> 00:11:05,220
But there is another area that gets heaps of hype that may be more justified.

95
00:11:05,614 --> 00:11:15,268
In fact, when I was in quantum computing, this was the area of quantum computing I was most excited for and which I was ultimately most disappointed by.

96
00:11:15,758 --> 00:11:17,280
It's quantum chemistry.

97
00:11:17,520 --> 00:11:19,972
Simulating molecules is a big deal.

98
00:11:20,240 --> 00:11:28,548
For example, in medicine, where you want to see how a particular drug is going to interact with, for example, proteins in your body.

99
00:11:28,880 --> 00:11:39,440
And so there was a lot of promise about quantum computers being able to speed up these very important chemical simulations.

100
00:11:39,600 --> 00:11:47,044
At the time that seemed justified to me because quantum computers are able to do something called ground state energy estimation.

101
00:11:47,342 --> 00:11:51,780
And they seem to do it exponentially more quickly than a classical computer.

102
00:11:52,334 --> 00:11:59,172
But it turned out there's actually an assumption in there that kind of makes the whole thing invalid.

103
00:11:59,598 --> 00:12:00,640
So let me explain.

104
00:12:01,070 --> 00:12:09,060
Say you had these two chemicals and you want to know what's the chance that they're going to bond together, and how hard is it going to be to make them do that.

105
00:12:09,360 --> 00:12:14,852
One way to get a handle on that is to estimate the energy of both of these states.

106
00:12:15,662 --> 00:12:22,304
So you can look at this state and you can look at its energy, and you can look at this whole state and look at its energy.

107
00:12:22,384 --> 00:12:29,060
And if this energy is significantly lower than that one, then it's more likely to end up in this state.

108
00:12:29,358 --> 00:12:33,024
Estimating these ground state energies is super important.

109
00:12:33,550 --> 00:12:38,724
And there's a quantum algorithm called phase estimation that seems to do it super well.

110
00:12:39,150 --> 00:12:40,196
This is the gist of it.

111
00:12:40,558 --> 00:12:49,668
You put in a blank qubit that you're going to write the answer onto, and you also put in this qubit which is in the ground state.

112
00:12:49,904 --> 00:12:57,988
Now, if you have this ground state and the blank qubit, you can just put them straight into your quantum computer that does phase estimation.

113
00:12:58,318 --> 00:13:05,584
And then the blank qubit ends up coming out of this computation with the ground state energy just written straight onto it.

114
00:13:05,710 --> 00:13:08,452
And you can read that out and find out what the energy was.

115
00:13:09,102 --> 00:13:13,424
So this seems perfect because this algorithm runs really fast.

116
00:13:13,806 --> 00:13:20,100
And so it seems like you can find out the ground state energy of any molecules, which would be huge.

117
00:13:20,622 --> 00:13:23,812
But unfortunately, the trick is this thing.

118
00:13:24,366 --> 00:13:31,876
So this is a state, a qubit state, which represents the ground state of the molecules.

119
00:13:32,144 --> 00:13:39,940
In other words, you need to already know what the ground state of those molecules is to be able to run this algorithm.

120
00:13:40,462 --> 00:13:41,860
You can kind of see the problem, right?

121
00:13:42,190 --> 00:13:49,504
But at the time I thought that this would be fine because there are ways to estimate what the ground state would look like more or less.

122
00:13:49,742 --> 00:13:58,148
And as long as there's a good overlap between your guess and the reality, then you end up with something that is good enough to find the energy.

123
00:13:58,510 --> 00:14:03,716
But there's no guarantee that your guess and the actual ground state will have all that much overlap.

124
00:14:04,270 --> 00:14:13,668
It was actually a couple of friends of mine who weren't in quantum computing but did know a whole lot about chemistry and biology that actually pointed this out to me.

125
00:14:14,190 --> 00:14:19,104
They made me see that there's actually no guarantee that this overlap is big.

126
00:14:19,310 --> 00:14:24,708
And so it's not clear at all that quantum computers can solve this ground state energy problem.

127
00:14:25,294 --> 00:14:32,356
And in fact, in 2022 there was a paper called Is There Evidence for Exponential Quantum Advantage?

128
00:14:32,864 --> 00:14:40,068
And it was written by a whole lot of very respected scientists in the quantum computing field and also in chemistry.

129
00:14:40,334 --> 00:14:42,244
And so their conclusion was this.

130
00:14:42,574 --> 00:14:48,784
We conclude that evidence for such an exponential advantage across chemical space has yet to be found.

131
00:14:49,166 --> 00:14:59,984
While quantum computers may still prove useful for quantum chemistry, it may be prudent to assume exponential speedups are not generically available for this problem.

132
00:15:00,398 --> 00:15:05,188
So yeah, quantum computers aren't actually a silver bullet for solving quantum chemistry problems.

133
00:15:05,678 --> 00:15:13,060
But there is a class of problems that's quite related that quantum computers are surprisingly good at, and that's quantum simulations.

134
00:15:13,614 --> 00:15:16,900
Quantum simulations are actually what inspired quantum computing.

135
00:15:17,230 --> 00:15:18,244
The idea is simple.

136
00:15:18,574 --> 00:15:22,304
Quantum systems are actually very hard to simulate in general.

137
00:15:22,478 --> 00:15:32,544
If you try to, for example, simulate what a bunch of electrons are doing when they interact with each other quantum mechanically, you find that on a regular computer, this is really, really hard to do.

138
00:15:32,704 --> 00:15:42,020
The number of possible states inside of the superposition become exponential, and so it's hard even on a classical computer to write down all of these states.

139
00:15:42,544 --> 00:15:46,384
And so quantum simulation has always been a difficult problem.

140
00:15:46,926 --> 00:15:50,052
But for a quantum computer, it's actually quite easy.

141
00:15:50,414 --> 00:16:00,452
So what quantum computers can do is they can simulate the way that electrons evolve by having their qubits sort of interact with each other in a similar way.

142
00:16:00,814 --> 00:16:06,724
And by doing that analog, they can give us a pretty good picture of what the original electrons would have done.

143
00:16:07,278 --> 00:16:09,284
And this turns out to be very useful.

144
00:16:09,774 --> 00:16:13,060
So here are some examples of what we might do with quantum simulations.

145
00:16:13,582 --> 00:16:16,292
So you might have heard of these things called superconductors.

146
00:16:16,622 --> 00:16:21,764
Superconductors are these kind of magical materials that have no electrical resistance.

147
00:16:22,542 --> 00:16:26,404
That means that when you use them in a circuit, they don't lose energy.

148
00:16:26,862 --> 00:16:34,468
But the problem with superconductors is that the ones that we know of have to be cooled down quite a lot to actually work.

149
00:16:35,118 --> 00:16:39,396
So one thing people dream of is coming up with high temperature superconductors.

150
00:16:39,662 --> 00:16:49,636
So we just have to figure out the exact materials to use to make this superconductor so that it has zero resistance even at room temperature.

151
00:16:50,158 --> 00:16:57,764
And quantum computers might help with that because it's really hard to simulate these kind of materials before you actually make them.

152
00:16:58,062 --> 00:17:02,724
Because of those problems I was talking about earlier, it's hard to figure out what the electrons are all doing.

153
00:17:02,990 --> 00:17:08,580
And so the easiest way to do it at the moment is just to make all of these materials and then test them in the lab.

154
00:17:09,088 --> 00:17:13,540
But that's really difficult when there's so many different materials that you can make and test.

155
00:17:14,094 --> 00:17:28,324
So the idea is that you know, once we eventually have a quantum computer, we would be able to basically just computer simulate what all of these different materials are going to do and then just pick out the most promising candidates to use in the lab.

156
00:17:28,686 --> 00:17:30,916
And I think that that's a very exciting application.

157
00:17:31,406 --> 00:17:35,044
There are similar problems like this, like for example, solar cells.

158
00:17:35,502 --> 00:17:43,968
You might know that at the moment our solar cells are like somewhere around, I think, 20 to 25% efficient, which is pretty good.

159
00:17:44,110 --> 00:17:49,540
Like they can take 25% of the energy that falls on them and convert that into electricity.

160
00:17:50,048 --> 00:17:53,764
But that is actually near the limit of what's possible with silicon.

161
00:17:54,128 --> 00:18:00,688
The hope is that we'll be able to find different types of solar cells that have better efficiencies.

162
00:18:00,846 --> 00:18:04,484
And people have already started working on these and have examples of them.

163
00:18:04,848 --> 00:18:12,772
But hopefully, once we have quantum computers, we'll be able to simulate way more materials and find something with really high efficiency.

164
00:18:13,088 --> 00:18:21,888
Because computing the efficiency that a particular material is going to have before you actually make it in the lab and test it is again very hard.

165
00:18:22,094 --> 00:18:26,048
There's many other examples like that, like for example, nitrogen fixation.

166
00:18:26,128 --> 00:18:30,756
We want to figure out how we can do that much more efficiently than the current Harbor Brosch process.

167
00:18:31,008 --> 00:18:33,540
And there's also applications inside of science.

168
00:18:33,870 --> 00:18:39,844
So for example, you might have heard that quantum computers can be used to simulate some models of black holes.

169
00:18:40,128 --> 00:18:47,844
And that may be somewhat useful for you know people who are studying this to understand what kind of behavior those sort of black holes would have.

170
00:18:48,366 --> 00:18:54,688
So yeah, I think that there are actually a lot of applications in this area that are very promising.

171
00:18:54,862 --> 00:18:57,728
So that is great, but this isn't a new algorithm.

172
00:18:58,030 --> 00:19:01,060
Hamiltonian simulation is a fairly old algorithm.

173
00:19:01,454 --> 00:19:03,268
Where are the new algorithms?

174
00:19:03,662 --> 00:19:10,468
Well, amazingly and very unexpectedly, in the last five years there has been at least one new algorithm.

175
00:19:10,848 --> 00:19:23,812
So in 2023, a group of researchers proved that a particular problem with a random oracle could be solved on a quantum computer exponentially faster than could be solved on a classical one.

176
00:19:24,208 --> 00:19:26,852
That is really cool as a theoretical result.

177
00:19:27,328 --> 00:19:33,348
But this random oracle bit means that it can't actually be made in practice.

178
00:19:34,030 --> 00:19:47,648
Still, I am actually really hopeful about that because usually when there are algorithms of this sort where they're sort of relative to some Oracle, often people come up with a way to take that idea and put it into a practical algorithm.

179
00:19:47,918 --> 00:19:52,676
Like I think Shaw's algorithm even came out of sort of oracle-like results like that.

180
00:19:53,166 --> 00:19:58,368
So that's proof that there really are other quantum algorithms out there, and I think it's really exciting.

181
00:19:58,638 --> 00:20:05,124
If you work in quantum computing, you might have worried that like most of my videos sounded quite negative, and I'm really sorry about that.

182
00:20:05,328 --> 00:20:08,228
I actually don't want to come down too hard on quantum computing.

183
00:20:08,528 --> 00:20:20,932
I just think that there has been a bit of a misallocation where a lot of work has, of course, gone into the hardware because it's a very hard problem, and a lot of work has gone into, for example, quantum error correction.

184
00:20:21,328 --> 00:20:25,168
But it just seems like quantum algorithms have been neglected.

185
00:20:25,486 --> 00:20:31,408
If a quantum computer appeared tomorrow, we need things that we're going to use that quantum computer for.

186
00:20:31,662 --> 00:20:34,564
So we need to be searching for good quantum algorithms.

187
00:20:35,022 --> 00:20:40,388
But at least when I was in the field, it wasn't very popular to work on quantum algorithms.

188
00:20:40,750 --> 00:20:48,164
And that's because it had a reputation for being really hard, which makes sense, because so few algorithms have been found.

189
00:20:48,448 --> 00:20:56,448
And even when new algorithms are found, often there's a mistake with them, or people show that actually classical computers can do that too.

190
00:20:56,654 --> 00:20:59,396
And so it's quite a hard field to be working in.

191
00:20:59,726 --> 00:21:01,444
But I think it's one of the most important.

192
00:21:01,998 --> 00:21:08,068
So yeah, I'm hopeful about quantum computers, but I think that there is a lot of work left to be done.

